ralph/progress.txt:57:- Verification-suite single-file multi-surface static-test, chaos-Postgres-disconnects instance (BE-0394 pattern, internal/release/verification_suite_chaos_postgres_disconnects_static_test.go + internal/controlplane/store/chaos_postgres_disconnects_test.go's new canonical pair TestChaosPostgresDisconnectsCoversCallSites + TestChaosPostgresDisconnectsMapsToTypedUnavailableEnvelope + new internal/controlplane/store/fakepg package (Server + DisconnectFault + NewServer) + SECURITY.md "## Chaos Tests For Postgres Disconnects" section AND a new row in the Required Verification Gates table + internal/release/AGENTS.md "## Verification suite: chaos tests for Postgres disconnects (BE-0394)" subsection; SIXTEENTH worked instance of the BE-0379..BE-0393 verification-suite mega-pattern, canonical command `go test -run TestChaosPostgresDisconnects ./...`, CI step name `Chaos tests for Postgres disconnects`, verify.sh prefix #19 inserted between BE-0393's #18 and the trailing optionals renumbered 19->20/20->21/21->22/22->23 in the same edit. Per-story knobs unchanged from BE-0393's bullet — (a) canonical command from PRD, (b) dedicated CI step name, (c) verify.sh numeric prefix (next number after last required step; renumber trailing optionals in same edit), (d) SECURITY.md section heading and substring set (BE-0394 substring count = 16; matches BE-0393's count), (e) AGENTS.md subsection mirroring SECURITY.md but speaking to maintainers. Load-bearing invariant unchanged: ONE FILE, SIX SURFACES, SELF-CHECK; drift on any surface fails ONE test, not six. Three BE-0394-specific extensions to the chaos-classification template, all forward-applicable to ANY future chaos-Postgres / chaos-network-partition / chaos-rate-limit sibling whose subject is the store layer or any pgxpool-backed dependency: (1) `NEW IN-PROCESS FAKE NETWORK ENDPOINT FOR PGX` — unlike BE-0393's dokployfake (which exists as a long-standing HTTP test double under internal/controlplane/dokploy/dokployfake), there was no fakepg before this story. The minimal viable shape is a TCP listener on 127.0.0.1:0 that does NOT speak the Postgres wire protocol — it accepts the TCP connection (so pgx's dial completes successfully — important: graceful FIN, NOT RST via SetLinger(0), because RST races the dial on some kernels and surfaces as `connection reset by peer` on connect rather than the production failure mode of "dial succeeds, handshake fails") and then closes the conn, so pgx observes EOF on its startup-handshake read. The fake exposes a FIFO Fault queue (only DisconnectFault for now), a Host()/Port()/Addr() trio for DSN construction, an AcceptedConnections() counter so the harness can assert pgx made >=1 TCP attempt per op (a regression that short-circuited pgx without ever attempting a network connect would otherwise pass silently), Close() (idempotent), New(testing.TB) for tests inside the testing package, and NewServer() error for worker-goroutine constructors that cannot satisfy testing.TB. Forward-applicable to every future chaos-Postgres sibling: add SQLErrorFault (writes an ErrorResponse before closing — would require pgproto3 dependency from pgx), LatencyFault (delays the close), AuthFailFault (writes a FATAL ErrorResponse during startup). (2) `CLASSIFIER-WRAP CONTRACT, NOT END-TO-END CALLSITE WRAP` — unlike BE-0393's chaos test which exercises real production methods on the typed Dokploy client (c.GetDeployment, c.EnsureOrganization, c.RemoveService) so the test asserts on errors the client's PRODUCTION wrapping chokepoint already produced, BE-0394's chaos test exercises raw pgxpool ops (Pool.Ping, Pool.Acquire, Pool.Begin, Pool.Exec, Pool.Query) and EXPLICITLY wraps the resulting pgx error through apierr.StoreUnavailable inside the runtime member before asserting the contract. This is because the store layer does not currently route every call site through a single classifier chokepoint the way the Dokploy client does — store call sites return raw pgx errors and the wrapping happens at handler-shim boundaries. The chaos contract therefore pins the wrapper's behaviour given realistic pgx-derived errors, not every call site's wrapping behaviour. Forward-applicable to any verification story whose subject lacks a single production classifier chokepoint: assert the wrapper given realistic causes, not the production path. When a future story (BE-04xx) adds a store-layer classifier chokepoint, the chaos test can switch to invoking that chokepoint instead — the scenario table and the redaction/dependency/code assertions stay identical. (3) `ASYMMETRIC REDACTION CONTRACT — PASSWORD AT EVERY LEVEL, USERNAME AT WRAPPER ONLY` — pgx's connect error formatter intentionally includes the DSN username in error messages (for operator debuggability) but redacts the password. The chaos test's chaosPostgresSecretMarkers (cause-chain scan) therefore lists ONLY the password sentinel; the wrapper-envelope assertion (chaosPostgresEnvelopeMarkers) lists BOTH the password and the username sentinel because apierr.StoreUnavailable's rendered message is static ("the Yalla datastore is temporarily unavailable") and MUST NOT echo ANY pgx cause-chain content. The runtime member walks errors.Unwrap chain for the password marker AND asserts the wrapper envelope's rendered Error() string for both markers. Forward-applicable: any future chaos test whose subject is a wire-protocol library that includes debug info in errors should split the redaction contract into chain-level (real secrets only) + envelope-level (anything pgx-style libraries might leak into debug strings). The sentinel-literal approach (chaosPostgresSentinelPassword="yallachaossecret_DO_NOT_LEAK_42") keeps the assertion meaningful: if the wrapper EVER started embedding the cause's message, the sentinel would surface and the gate would trip on the first observation. Per-iteration setup cost is bounded by chaosPostgresConnectTimeout=750ms (per-attempt pgx connect deadline) which is strictly less than chaosPostgresOpTimeout=2s (per-op context deadline), so the burst (5 scenarios × 4 workers × 3 iterations = 60 iterations) completes in ~0.5s wall-clock.
ralph/progress.txt:116:- Log-redaction fuzz harness, single-test runtime-only variant (BE-0359 pattern, internal/output/redact_fuzz_test.go + SECURITY.md "## Log Redaction Fuzzing" section + internal/output/AGENTS.md "Log redaction fuzzing (BE-0359)" subsection; FIRST worked instance of the single-test runtime-only shape called out in the BE-0344 pattern's closing line — applies to verification stories whose invariant is a runtime contract with NO static analog, e.g. log redaction fuzzing, idempotency replay, chaos tests for timeouts). Six load-bearing rules: (1) FUZZ-MARKER design — when proving a structural redaction rule scrubs the value-half of a transport pattern, bracket the fuzz-supplied value with a unique fixed marker (`"FUZZAUTHMARKERXYZ"`, `"FUZZQRYMARKERZYX"`) and assert the marker never appears in the redacted output. The marker is the leak detector, independent of the value's bytes. Asserting that the value-bytes do not appear false-positives whenever the value happens to equal a substring of the test's static suffix (e.g. `"example"` if the test constructs `Host: example.com`). The marker pattern is reusable for ANY future fuzz target that proves "structural rule X scrubs the value half of transport Y" — cookies, refresh tokens, future header/query patterns. (2) REGEX-VALUE-CLASS gotcha — Go's `regexp` `\s` is exactly `[\t\n\f\r ]`; vertical-tab (`\v`) is NOT in `\s` but form-feed (`\f`) IS. When constructing a fuzz value that must traverse a regex value class without being cut short, strip every character in the regex's exclusion set including every member of Go's `\s`. The first revision missed `\f` and a seed containing it ended the regex mid-value, surviving the marker bracket and producing a false-positive leak. (3) DEFENCE-IN-DEPTH interactions — when two structural rules can both fire on a fuzz value (e.g. a query value containing `AUTHORIZATION:` triggers the bearer regex which eats the rest of the URL), assert ONLY the safety property (marker absent), NOT cosmetic properties (non-secret params survive). Document the interaction in the test file so a future reader understands why the survival assertion is absent. (4) SENTINEL-OVERLAP scope-out — `Redactor.Redact` uses `strings.ReplaceAll(s, secret, Sentinel)`; if `secret` is a substring of `Sentinel` (e.g. `"REDA"` inside `"[REDACTED]"`), the second pass rewrites the freshly-inserted sentinel substring and the function is NOT idempotent. Skip these inputs in fuzz AND pin the exclusion in a regression test (`TestRedactor_FuzzScopeExclusionsAreReal`) so a future redactor fix (single-pass regex substitution that does not re-scan substituted text) deliberately breaks that test and forces a coordinated update to the fuzz scope, the SECURITY.md section, and the AGENTS.md note. (5) SEED-CORPUS vs `-fuzz` — `go test ./...` runs every seed in `f.Add` once, so the seed corpus is the regression net every commit exercises; `-fuzz` is operator-opt-in. When adding a new redaction rule, the worked pattern is: append a seed input to `redactFuzzSeeds` that triggers the new rule, add a fuzz target if the rule has a distinct structural shape, and update both the AGENTS.md and SECURITY.md sections at the same time. (6) `-fuzztime` SHUTDOWN quirk — `go test -fuzz=… -fuzztime=5s` with the default 16 workers sometimes reports `context deadline exceeded` at shutdown; this is a harness-internal timing quirk, not a real fuzz failure (no `testdata/fuzz/…` corpus file is written). Re-running with `-parallel=2` makes the shutdown cleaner. Forward-applicable to any future security-verification story whose invariant has no AST shape to gate (BE-0360 environment-variable redaction if the rule is purely "the variables service MUST pass every rendered value through the redactor before logging" with no AST shape; BE-0363 rate-limit bypass resistance; chaos-tests for timeouts; idempotency replay).
ralph/progress.txt:129:- BE-0303 application-image confirms the inverted-boundary clone of the BE-0301 pattern (internal/controlplane/dokploy/renderer_application_image_test.go): a single-builder subtype (Builder == BuilderImage) collapses the BE-0301 builderCase outer loop to a single role-only loop, swaps DropsImageField for DropsGitFields (submits leaky dockerfile_path + git_branch + git_commit and asserts every one is dropped AND never appears in Summary JSON / slog record / Summary.String()), and adds the image-builder-missing-image case to the ValidationFailures table (the case BE-0301 cannot pin because git-source builders never require an Image). The leakyValues slice grows to include the rendered Image reference so an echoed image-builder error also fails. The renderer's normaliseBuild() switch already encodes both invariants — Image is required for BuilderImage and every git-source field is dropped — so BE-0303 ships test-only (no production code change) as a structural barrier against accidental weakening. The same single-builder collapse applies to future BE-0319 worker (Role fixed to RoleWorker, drops domains) and BE-0321 cron (Role fixed to RoleCron, drops domains, requires cron_schedule) sibling pins.
ralph/progress.txt:130:- BE-0319 worker is the FIRST role-axis clone of the BE-0301 pattern (internal/controlplane/dokploy/renderer_worker_test.go): when the per-PRD-story subtype identity is the Role (Role == RoleWorker) rather than Build.Builder, Service.Type, or Service.Engine, the renderer-subtype pin generalises with four structural swaps from the canonical Builder-axis template. (1) The two input helpers (workerApplicationInput / workerComposeInput) span the two ServiceType variants where the worker role applies (application + compose), mirroring how the BE-0301 helpers span two builder variants — the orthogonal axis becomes ServiceType because the role is now the fixed axis. Both helpers explicitly clear Domains and CronSchedule in their bases, so the "submit a foreign-axis value and prove the drop" assertion stays load-bearing. (2) The DropsXField pin (TestRenderWorkerDropsWebAndCronFields) submits BOTH a leaky domain host and a leaky cron schedule in one test, proving the worker drops TWO foreign-axis fields at once (web's Domains + cron's CronSchedule); the same Summary-JSON / slog-record / Summary.String() redaction sweep used by BE-0303 catches leaks of either value. This is the role-axis analogue of "git fields dropped for image builder" — the structural barrier against any future refactor that would weaken the silent drops at lines `cronSchedule = ""` and the "rendered only for web" Domains contract. (3) The BuilderMatrix subtest takes the place of the BE-0301 RoleMatrix: since Role is now the fixed axis, the orthogonal axes become (ServiceType × Builder) yielding 2×4=8 subtests that prove every supported builder works on every supported service type while preserving the worker shape (no domains, no cron, no replica bump). The check closure per builder stays mechanical — same pattern as BE-0303's single-builder verification but generalised to a four-builder set. (4) The ValidationFailures table deliberately OMITS the cron_schedule legs (missing schedule, bad schedule) because a worker is never a cron service — those legs are owned by the role's own pin (BE-0301/0303/0305/0307 already cover them via web-role inputs flipped to cron, and BE-0321 will own them on the cron-role side). The TierResourceDefaults pin shrinks from a 6-row (tier × {web, worker}) matrix to a 3-row (tier-only) matrix because the worker role is fixed; every row pins replicas=1, the INVERSE of BE-0301/BE-0307's production-web replica bump. Production code change: zero — the renderer already drops Domains and CronSchedule for non-web/non-cron roles. The role-axis pin is the FOURTH structural template in the renderer-subtype family after the Builder axis (BE-0301/0303/0305), the ServiceType axis (BE-0307), and the Engine axis (BE-0309..0317). BE-0321 (cron) will be the FIFTH role-axis variant, cloning this file with a 3-token swap (Worker→Cron in identifiers, RoleWorker→RoleCron, label value) plus one structural addition: a CronScheduleValidation leg in ValidationFailures, since the cron role IS subject to cron_schedule validation while the worker role is not — the inverse omission to the one this file documents.
ralph/progress.txt:131:- BE-0307 compose is the FIRST type-boundary clone of the BE-0301 pattern (internal/controlplane/dokploy/renderer_compose_test.go): when the per-PRD-story subtype identity is the ServiceType ITSELF (Service.Type == ServiceCompose) rather than the Build.Builder, the seven-pin template is preserved with three structural swaps. (1) Input helpers (composeDockerfileInput / composeNixpacksInput) carry the SAME two git builders as application-git — the boundary is NOT what the builder is — and the only difference vs applicationGit*Input is `in.Service.Type = dokploy.ServiceCompose`. (2) The Drops* test becomes a SubtypeBoundaryFromApplication test that renders BOTH composeDockerfileInput() AND applicationGitDockerfileInput() side-by-side and asserts each rendered Service.Type matches its input verbatim — neither is silently coerced — AND that the `yalla.service-type` label in spec.Service.Labels reflects the same value, because that label is the audit/observability surface a downstream consumer queries to distinguish a compose stack from a single-container application without re-reading the spec body; an alternate seam-coverage shape preserves the structural barrier when there is no foreign-builder-only field to drop. (3) The ValidationFailures table expands to cover EVERY builder's failure path that the subtype can reach — compose may use any of dockerfile/nixpacks/image/drop-artifact, so BE-0307's table includes BOTH the image-builder-missing-image case (from BE-0303) AND the drop-artifact-builder-missing-artifact-url case (from BE-0305) PLUS the bad-builder case — the leakyValues slice grows to carry the rendered Image AND ArtifactURL references so any echo from those branches fails too. The RoleMatrix / TierResourceDefaults / Redaction / Determinism pins keep the seven-pin shape intact, but RoleMatrix and the per-test invariants assert `Service.Type == ServiceCompose && Image == "" && ArtifactURL == "" && Engine == ""` (engine is database-only — that is the OTHER per-type seam BE-0309..0317 will pin). The load-bearing production-web 2-replica default participates here too: defaultResources() in renderer.go gates the bump on `(typ == ServiceApplication || typ == ServiceCompose)`, so TestRenderComposeTierResourceDefaults pins the compose half of that disjunction directly — a future regression that narrowed the disjunction to ServiceApplication-only would flip this pin. Forward-applicable to ANY future per-service-type subtype whose differentiator is the ServiceType itself (currently only compose vs application; future "function" / "edge" / similar would clone this template); NOT applicable to the BE-0309..0317 database engines, which pin Engine instead of either ServiceType or Builder, drop the role field entirely (effectiveRole := "" for ServiceDatabase in renderer.go:351), and need a database variant whose RoleMatrix collapses to a single (no-role) case and whose Engine-required + Image-dropped invariants supersede the Build.Builder switch's normalisation. The test-only-no-prod-code-change shape from BE-0303 is preserved — BE-0307 ships purely as a structural barrier against accidental weakening of the renderer's existing service-type discrimination.
ralph/progress.txt:194:- HTTP idempotency (0009_idempotency_keys, store/idempotency.go, httpapi/idempotency.go): mutating endpoints are made retry-safe by httpapi.RequireIdempotency(IdempotencyStore, ttl) — middleware installed INSIDE RequireAuth (it reads policy.PrincipalFromContext to scope the key to a principal within a tenant). Safe methods and unsafe requests with no Idempotency-Key pass straight through (opt-in). The claim protocol: store.IdempotencyRepository.Claim is a WHERE-guarded upsert — INSERT ... ON CONFLICT DO UPDATE ... WHERE expires_at <= now() — so it freshly claims, transparently steals an expired row (claimed=true), or returns the existing live claim (claimed=false, RETURNING yields no row, follow-up Find reads the row the DO UPDATE attempt locked). On claimed=true the middleware runs the handler against a buffering responseCapture, then Complete records the rendered envelope (status < 500) or Release drops the claim (status >= 500, so a server failure is retried not replayed). On claimed=false: hash mismatch -> apierr.IdempotencyConflict (E_IDEMPOTENCY_CONFLICT, the only use of the new code), completed -> replay the recorded body+status with Idempotency-Replayed: true, still pending -> apierr.Conflict "already in progress". The request hash is sha256(method\npath?query\nbody); the body is buffered then restored as a fresh r.Body so the handler still reads it. Keys are scoped UNIQUE (organization_id, principal_id, idempotency_key); a completion CHECK ties status='completed' to a non-null response. The store-backed IdempotencyStore adapter (httpapi.NewIdempotencyStore) runs each repo call in its own Store.Write — RequireIdempotency is implemented and tested but not yet wired into a route (no mutating endpoints exist yet), same as RequireAuth.
ralph/progress.txt:235:- POST create endpoint for a service-scoped child resource (BE-0250+0251+0252 pattern, POST /v1/services/{service_id}/backups, mirrors the service_domains POST trio BE-0238+0239+0240; clonable for BE-0264 previews, BE-0289 import previews, and any future bare-id child create): the store-layer composition is the canonical create-unit-of-work shape — ServiceXxxRepository.Insert (single-statement INSERT...RETURNING + mapWriteError mapping driver errors to typed apierr.Conflict whose message is value-free) + ServiceXxxService.Create orchestrator that opens a Store.Write Tx, runs the tenant-scoped parent existence check first (so a cross-tenant or unknown parent id surfaces as a deterministic apierr.NotFound, NEVER a 500 or silent success), runs an in-tx Authorize (defence-in-depth against grant-change races between the HTTP authorize and the desired-state write — the ONLY operation that runs in-tx Authorize; the in-transaction Authorizer is reserved for Create), runs the Insert, and Appends the immutable audit record — all inside the same *Tx so a row can never be persisted without its audit trail. Validation runs BEFORE the Tx is opened (validateCreateXxxInput against a shared validate.Collector so a request rejecting on more than one field surfaces every violation in one typed apierr.InvalidInput; submitted values NEVER appear in violation reasons). The customer-facing create pins any lifecycle status column to a fixed initial value (e.g. ServiceBackupStatusPending) so a customer cannot forge a 'succeeded' / 'completed' status through the public API surface — the worker is the authority for closed-taxonomy transitions; the status column is NOT in the createXxxRequest decoder shape and the validator overwrites in.Status in the validated struct. The audit Metadata records ONLY fields the application layer can prove redaction-safe by construction: structural identifiers (service_id), closed-taxonomy enums (status, certificate_type), and bounded primitives (retention_count, version, enabled flag rendered as "true"/"false"); caller-supplied free text (display_name, schedule, hostname even — schedule is a cron-style expression but is still caller-controlled bytes) is NEVER recorded verbatim, because a customer pasting a token into one of those fields is exactly the footgun a defensive convention avoids. The httpapi-layer composition is the canonical create-handler shape — createXxxRequest decoder (Enabled / boolean-default-true fields are pointers to preserve the absent-vs-explicit-false distinction at the JSON seam — `*bool` decoded; an omitted field collapses to the default at the handler boundary, an explicit `false` rides through unchanged; integer fields take their schema-side default when zero) + createXxxPayload + createXxxHandler that gates on RequireAuth + the typed-internal nil-creator guard + validate.DecodeJSON (strict, max-body-bytes, unknown-field rejection, no input echo on parse failure) + creator.Create + http.StatusCreated yalla.output.v1 envelope projecting through serviceXxxOf. Action is CapWrite (ActionXxxCreate -> CapWrite in catalog.go): owner / admin / developer allowed by role; CI / viewer / support denied via ReasonDeniedNoCapability — load-bearing distinction: a CapDeploy sibling action (e.g. ActionBackupRun, ActionDeploymentCreate) WOULD admit CI. The wire route uses serviceIDResolver (no parent_project_id leg pinned on the resource scope), so EVERY project-/env-/service-scoped grant is denied at the boundary via the engine's one-way covers() rule (ReasonDeniedOutOfScope); only an organization-wide write role or an org-level write grant reaches the handler. Cross-tenant: the resolver pins resource scope to the principal's home org id (never the path service's tenant), so cross-tenant service_ids hit the persistence layer with the home org id and surface as a deterministic apierr.NotFound — engine cross-tenant deny is pinned at the engine level (defence-in-depth: EVERY role including Support is denied cross-tenant for CapWrite — the support cross-tenant exception covers only CapRead / CapSupport). Contract test surface: success with full plumbing assertions (principal home org id forwarded, path service id forwarded, actor id forwarded, no caller-body org id trusted), boolean-default-true and explicit-false at the pointer seam, request_id propagation, validation failure propagated from creator as E_INVALID_INPUT (handler delegates validation to the store-layer orchestrator), strict-decode unknown-field rejection BEFORE creator runs, unauthenticated 401, role-deny 403 (handler never reached), cross-tenant 404 with principal home org forwarded, duplicate-id Conflict 409, store-unavailable 503, nil-creator typed Internal 500. Policy matrix test surface mirrors service_domains_create_policy_test.go one-for-one. Wiring footprint: adds ONE new positional arg (ServiceXxxCreator) to newRouteTable (~slot 57) and NewHandler (~slot 60); ~70 test files / ~120 call sites need a `fakeServiceXxxCreator{}` insertion via bulk substitution `fakeServiceYyyReader{}, fakeServiceZzzReader{}` -> `fakeServiceYyyReader{}, fakeServiceXxxCreator{}, fakeServiceZzzReader{}` (where Yyy is the sibling Reader and Zzz is the next port after the new slot in the signature); the special cases are the in-package contract-test helper for the new resource (`reader` -> `..., reader, fakeServiceXxxCreator{}, ...`) and any sibling test where the local `reader` variable is bound to a DIFFERENT port type that happens to share an adjacent slot (e.g. service_variables_test.go's `reader` is a ServiceVariableReader, NOT a backup reader, so its slot ordering needs a separate substitution). Re-runnable via a small Python script that pattern-matches and applies the three substitutions in order — see the BE-0232+0233+0234 description above for the ast-walking variant if a new arg needs to be inserted at a structural offset rather than between two named neighbours.
