71:- Security-verification cross-package secret-confinement pattern, Dokploy-token instance (BE-0361 pattern, internal/release/dokploy_token_isolation_static_test.go + internal/controlplane/config/dokploy_token_isolation_test.go + SECURITY.md "## Dokploy Token Isolation" section + internal/controlplane/dokploy/AGENTS.md "Token isolation (BE-0361)" subsection; SEVENTH worked instance of the BE-0344 two-test template and FIRST cross-package instance — confirms the template scales from "this single function MUST honour rule X" (BE-0346) and "this directory MUST NOT import surface Y" (BE-0357 admin endpoints, BE-0349 SSRF) to "this scarce in-process secret MUST flow from source-of-truth package A through redaction package B into wire-emission package C with NO leak surface in customer-facing package D". Six load-bearing rules: (1) TRAILING-SELECTOR-NAME matching — when the field name is unambiguously the secret in the codebase ("DokployToken" is unique to the Config secret field, no other struct has a field of the same name), match by `SelectorExpr.Sel.Name == "DokployToken"` regardless of the receiver. This catches `config.DokployToken`, `cfg.DokployToken`, `c.DokployToken`, and any future-receiver shape alike, in ONE matcher rule, where the package-qualified-only variant would miss the `c.DokployToken` form a method using a `*config.Config` receiver would naturally write. The receiver-name freedom is the load-bearing rule: any closed list of qualifiers (`config.`, `cfg.`, …) is brittle to local-naming choices in a future refactor. (2) RETURN-FALSE-FROM-INSPECT to suppress double-counting when a matcher fires on a parent node whose children would ALSO match — `config.EnvDokployToken` is both a SelectorExpr (X=`config`, Sel=`EnvDokployToken`) and contains an inner `EnvDokployToken` Ident that the bare-Ident matcher would also flag. Returning `false` from the `ast.Inspect` callback after emitting the SelectorExpr diagnostic stops descent and keeps the count at 1. The self-check fixture `handler that reads config.EnvDokployToken is rejected` pins this — without the `return false` it would fail with `got 2, want 1`. (3) COUNT-AND-PIN-LOCATION instead of count-only — for invariants where the secret MUST appear at exactly one structural location (here, `c.token` in `client.go`'s Authorization header), the matcher returns the full set of `file:line:col` triples across every production file in the package, the test asserts `len == 1` AND the single hit's path-prefix equals the canonical file. A count-only assertion would let a future refactor move the line to a different file silently; a path-only assertion would let a duplicate slip in. The two assertions in series are strictly stronger than either alone. The constructor's `token: token` binding (a `KeyValueExpr.Key`, not a `SelectorExpr`) is NOT counted by construction — `ast.Inspect` walks the Key as an `Ident`, not a `SelectorExpr`, so the matcher's `SelectorExpr` pivot skips it for free. The self-check fixture `constructor binding token: token is NOT counted` pins this distinction. (4) COMPOSITE-LITERAL-FIELD-VALUE inspection for redaction-seam invariants — `(*Config).Redacted()` returns a `RedactedConfig{...}` composite literal whose `DokployToken` field value MUST be a CallExpr to the local `redact(...)` closure. The matcher uses `findMethodOnReceiver(file, "Config", "Redacted")` (a small helper that walks `file.Decls` filtering `FuncDecl.Recv.List[0].Type` against both `*ast.Ident` and `*ast.StarExpr` receivers), then walks the method body looking for ANY composite literal containing a `DokployToken` KeyValueExpr, then asserts the value is a CallExpr with `Fun.Name == "redact"`. Three orthogonal regressions fire one diagnostic each: missing method, missing field in the literal, bare-selector value instead of a call. The same pattern applies to ANY future "method M MUST stamp field F through helper H before returning" structural rule. (5) USES-FIRST gate for defence-in-depth log surfaces — `(*Config).LogValue()` MUST call `c.Redacted()` first AND MUST NOT touch `c.DokployToken` directly. The matcher walks the method body, tracking `seenRedactedCall` (set when a `c.Redacted()` CallExpr is found) and emitting a diagnostic for EVERY `c.DokployToken` SelectorExpr seen. The two diagnostics are independent — a method that called `c.Redacted()` but ALSO read `c.DokployToken` directly would fire one; a method that omitted the call but had no direct read would fire the other; a method that did both would fire both. Forward-applicable to any "MUST consult helper H before reading secret S" pattern (variables LogValue, future credentials redactor, etc.). (6) MARKER-PATTERN runtime gate at the projection seams — three table-driven tests in `dokploy_token_isolation_test.go` install a fixed `BE0361DOKPLOYTOKENMARKERXYZ` prefix on the Config.DokployToken field and assert the marker never appears in (a) the `json.Marshal(c.Redacted())` byte stream, (b) the `slog.JSONHandler` output under `slog.Any("config", c)`, and (c) the `c.String()` debug projection — for EACH of three fixtures: short-token (canonical), long-token (longer than the redactor's sentinel, longer than any reasonable line-buffered slog chunk so a partial-flush leak would still surface), and collision-prefix (token prefix collides with the projection's field name `dokploy_token`, so a regression that truncated at the field-name boundary would still trip the marker check). The marker is the leak detector — asserting on token bytes alone would false-positive whenever the value happens to equal a substring of the stable projection text. (7) GOLANGCI-LINT S1025 gotcha — staticcheck (under golangci-lint v2) flags `fmt.Sprintf("%s", x)` as redundant when `x` has a `String()` method, suggesting `x.String()` instead. For BE-0361 the intent of the runtime test was to PROVE that `fmt.Sprintf("%s", cfg)` doesn't leak, but since `fmt.Sprintf` with `%s` against a Stringer DELEGATES to `.String()`, calling `